body:prose = >`
  &ND v2

  # Aeonic Rational Arithmetic v1 Snapshot 0.2 [# aeonic-rational-arithmetic-v1-snapshot-0-2]

  Status: immutable compatibility snapshot aeonic-rational-arithmetic-specs-v1-snapshot-0.2,
  frozen 2026-11-14. Proposal lifecycle and disabled publication are preserved.
  The separate extended profile is [$ aeonic.operations.arithmetic.rational.v1].
  It must be explicitly selected through context or operation policy. The existing
  finite profile/default and immutable comparison/arithmetic snapshots are unchanged.
  This document introduces no AEON fraction literal, syntax extension or release.

  The [@ /artifacts/assets/aeonic-operations/v1/snapshots/0.2/rational-arithmetic-contract.schema.json | rational arithmetic schema]
  describes closed shapes. Arithmetic coprimality, staged errors and resource rules
  below are additional normative requirements that JSON Schema cannot fully express.

  ## 1. Domain and portable values [# 1-domain]

  Required binary operations are [$ add], [$ subtract], [$ multiply], [$ divide].
  Required unary operations are [$ quantize] and [$ materialize]. Every pairing of
  supported finite and derived operands is required. All calculation is exact for
  the supplied numerical operands; only explicit quantization changes a value by
  rounding. No host float, implicit coercion, NaN, infinity, aggregate, radix or
  temporal operation is introduced. One mathematical zero has no arithmetic sign.

  Requests contain exactly [$ operation] and [$ input]. Binary inputs require
  [$ left], then [$ right], and permit [$ context], [$ policy]. Unary inputs require
  [$ value] and permit [$ context], [$ policy]; their policy is required at stage 3.
  Operand bundles contain [$ number] and optional or required [$ history] as below.
  Request, input, supported descriptor, bundle, context, policy, metadata and result
  objects are closed. Error messages and CTS transport extensions are the only
  separately specified extensibility here. Applications may explicitly capture a
  diagnostic elsewhere; this contract never automatically turns an error into a value.

  Authored [$ finiteNumber] has exactly [$ category], [$ value]. Value is ASCII
  numerical text: optional sign; integer without leading zeros, optionally followed
  by a decimal point and one or more digits, or a leading point and digits; optional
  [$ e] or [$ E], optional exponent sign, and exponent digits. Examples are [$ 1],
  [$ 1.0], [$ 1e0], [$ .5], [$ -0]. Underscores are removed by a source adapter before
  this boundary, retaining original spelling separately. Native numbers are not text.
  Input representation distinctions are not mathematical distinctions.

  Derived [$ exactNumber] has [$ category], [$ coefficient], [$ exponent] and denotes
  [$ coefficient * 10^exponent]. Both integer fields are text. A nonzero coefficient
  has an optional minus sign, no plus sign, no leading zeros and no trailing zero.
  Exponent is [$ 0] or a signed-negative/unsigned-positive nonzero decimal integer,
  without leading zeros or plus sign. Zero is exactly coefficient [$ 0], exponent [$ 0].

  Derived [$ exactRational] additionally has [$ denominator] and denotes
  [$ coefficient * 10^exponent / denominator]. Coefficient is nonzero and follows
  the same rules. Denominator is unsigned positive decimal text, greater than one,
  without leading zeros, and relatively prime to ten. Coefficient and denominator
  are relatively prime. These conditions select exactly one triple per non-terminating
  rational value. For example, one sixth is coefficient [$ 5], exponent [$ -1],
  denominator [$ 3]. A finite decimal or zero uses [$ exactNumber], never a rational
  descriptor with denominator one or zero coefficient. Incoming derived descriptors
  must already be canonical: no silent admission normalization.

  Results whose exact value has a finite decimal expansion use [$ exactNumber].
  Other calculated results use [$ exactRational]. Normalization changes no value,
  chooses no Number output family, preserves history, and is subject to resource
  admission. Implementations may use different private representations.

  Recognized deferred categories are [$ string], [$ boolean], [$ symbol],
  [$ positiveInfinity], [$ negativeInfinity], [$ nan], [$ explicitNull], [$ explicitAbsence],
  [$ toggle], [$ hex], [$ radix], [$ encoding], [$ separator], [$ sansaAddress],
  [$ referenceForm], [$ temporal], [$ container]. Their descriptor payload is opaque
  beyond category. Their containing bundle/history remains validated. Unknown
  categories are malformed, not optional capabilities. All three supported kinds
  are compatible. Comparison support is independent and is not extended implicitly.

  ## 2. History, results and exactness [# 2-history]

  History has exactly [$ none-declared] and [$ approximation-declared]. It is optional
  for authored/deferred values, defaulting to [$ none-declared], and required for
  both derived kinds. Every success includes it. The marker is a declaration,
  not an error bound, provenance log, correlation model or accuracy certificate.
  Authored spelling never implies history. No declaration is a claim of no history.

  Binary arithmetic unions the two markers: any declared approximation propagates.
  Cancellation, multiplication by zero and later exact calculations never clear it.
  Quantization that changes a value sets [$ approximation-declared]; unchanged
  quantization and exact materialization preserve history. Bare Number export loses
  history unless the application explicitly maps it. No document annotation is added.

  Success contains exactly [$ outcome: "value"], [$ value], [$ metadata]. Value is a
  number/history bundle. Arithmetic/quantization produces a derived descriptor;
  materialization produces a [$ finiteNumber] descriptor. The actual returned bundle
  can feed a subsequent operation, without materializing, changing history or adding
  metadata fields to the operand bundle. Metadata has [$ evaluation], [$ effectivePolicy].
  Evaluation is [$ exact-for-operands] except value-changing quantization, which is
  [$ rounded-for-operands]. Thus one third rounded to [$ 0.33] is rounded, but its later
  multiplication by three is exact [$ 0.99] with declared approximation still present.

  ## 3. Explicit policies [# 3-policies]

  Context permits only optional nonempty Unicode-scalar [$ profile]. Binary policy
  permits only the same field. Quantization policy additionally requires [$ increment],
  then [$ rounding]. Increment is a positive canonical [$ exactNumber] descriptor:
  it has no history of its own and defines exact grid spacing. Authored text,
  zero/negative values, noncanonical pairs and rational increments are invalid policy.
  Rounding is one of the seven names in section 4, with no default or aliases.
  Materialization policy requires [$ family] in [$ integer], [$ decimal], [$ exponent],
  and permits [$ zeroSign] in [$ positive], [$ negative], default [$ positive]. It does
  not permit rounding/increment; quantization does not permit family/zeroSign.

  Validate all supplied context/policy shapes and field values before resolving
  availability of the effective profile. Policy profile overrides context profile.
  Only the effective valid profile is resolved; malformed overridden configuration
  still fails. With neither selection the effective identifier is the existing
  [$ aeonic.operations.arithmetic.exact.v1], not the extended identifier.

  The separate rational capability evaluates only its explicitly selected profile.
  Other effective identifiers yield [$ unsupported_profile]. An integrating facade
  dispatches the existing finite profile/default to the existing finite contract;
  it must preserve that contract's validation/diagnostics and claim separately.
  This lane does not reinterpret old requests under a widened default. A facade
  must not try a second profile after a failed evaluation. No implicit locale,
  environment, file, network, database, callback or policy lookup is performed.

  Effective policy metadata always includes the extended profile. Quantization
  includes the actual canonical increment and rounding name. Materialization includes
  the selected family and effective zeroSign. Binary metadata has no other fields.

  ## 4. Exact quantization [# 4-quantization]

  The grid is all integer multiples of positive finite decimal increment [$ h],
  with origin zero. Significant-digit rounding, nonzero offsets, fixed output scale
  and spelling preservation are deferred. Let [$ u = abs(x) / h = n + r], with
  nonnegative integer [$ n] and [$ 0 <= r < 1]. Choose nonnegative multiplier [$ k],
  restore the sign of [$ x], and multiply by [$ h]. All comparisons are exact.

  - On-grid [$ r = 0]: every mode selects [$ n].
  - [$ toward-zero]: select [$ n]. [$ away-from-zero]: select [$ n + 1] off-grid.
  - [$ floor]: off-grid, select [$ n] for positive input, [$ n + 1] for negative.
  - [$ ceiling]: off-grid, select [$ n + 1] for positive input, [$ n] for negative.
  - The three nearest modes select [$ n] for [$ 2r < 1], [$ n + 1] for [$ 2r > 1].
    At a tie, [$ half-even] selects the even multiplier, [$ half-away-from-zero]
    selects [$ n + 1], and [$ half-toward-zero] selects [$ n].

  At increment [$ 0.1], [$ -1.25] becomes [$ -1.2], [$ -1.3], [$ -1.2] under the three
  nearest tie modes respectively. Half-even maps [$ -1.35] to [$ -1.4]. At increment
  [$ 0.05], half-even maps [$ 0.125] to [$ 0.10], and [$ 0.175] to [$ 0.20]: parity
  concerns the grid multiplier, not an input's last digit. Every result is finite
  decimal and uses a normalized derived pair, not an already materialized Number.
  Rounding to zero produces mathematical zero, never an inherited negative zero.

  For fixed increment/mode, quantization is monotone and idempotent in value.
  Nearest error is at most half an increment; directed off-grid error is less than
  an increment. Floor/ceiling bracket the input. The nearest modes, toward-zero
  and away-from-zero respect sign symmetry; floor/ceiling are sign duals. History
  remains declared after re-quantization. These are semantic laws, not sorting APIs.

  ## 5. Exact materialization [# 5-materialization]

  Materialization is separate and never rounds. A rational descriptor cannot
  materialize as any finite Number family: return [$ unrepresentable_result] with
  no value. The original rational bundle remains usable by the application.
  For a canonical finite nonzero pair [$ (c, e)], let [$ a] be absolute coefficient
  text and [$ s] its sign. Render as follows after representability/length checks:

  - [$ integer]: require [$ e >= 0], otherwise [$ unrepresentable_result]. Append
    [$ e] zeros to [$ a] and restore sign.
  - [$ decimal]: for [$ e >= 0], append [$ e] zeros and [$ .0]. Otherwise put the
    point at position [$ len(a) + e], adding a leading [$ 0.] and fractional zeros
    where needed. Never emit exponent notation or redundant trailing zeros.
  - [$ exponent]: emit one leading coefficient digit and remaining digits after
    a point if present, then [$ e] and exponent [$ e + len(a) - 1] in canonical
    integer text. A one-digit coefficient has no point.

  Zero renders [$ 0], [$ 0.0], [$ 0e0] respectively. Explicit negative zeroSign
  prefixes minus on these zero renderings only. Nonzero signs never change.
  Destination schema admission is separate from exact rendering; this contract
  does not invent bounded integer storage or silently coerce a rejected Number.

  ## 6. Deterministic validation [# 6-validation]

  Fail at the first failed stage/substep. A diagnostic has exactly [$ outcome],
  [$ reason], [$ context], with optional scalar [$ message]. Context has [$ stage]
  and operation-local JSON Pointer [$ path], or null. No diagnostic has a value,
  partial result, warning success, automatic null or fallback profile.

  1. Request UTF-8 byte/depth guards precede deeper processing: [$ resource_limit],
     null path. Root container depth is one; logical JSON text uses its actual bytes.
  2. Strict JSON decoding, duplicate keys at every depth, request/input shapes,
     required operation/input and generic input vocabulary; then operation
     availability in this six-operation capability; then known-operation arity.
     Use [$ invalid_operand] or [$ unsupported_operation]. Generic input vocabulary
     is left, right, value, context, policy. A nonempty unknown operation precedes
     known-operation arity; unary policy is checked at stage 3, not arity.
     Operation names must be nonempty Unicode-scalar strings; malformed names
     are [$ invalid_operand] at [$ /operation].
  3. Context shape, policy shape/required fields, configuration values, then effective
     profile availability. Use [$ invalid_policy] or [$ unsupported_profile]. Validate
     context profile, policy profile, increment, rounding (or family, zeroSign) in
     that order. A reached numeric guard on increment is [$ resource_limit] at this
     stage. An absent explicit profile is unsupported here, with null profile path.
  4. Validate bundles/recognized categories/history and descriptors, left before
     right, or unary value. Required number/category and bundle fields precede history.
     Derived history is mandatory. Descriptor required/unknown fields precede payload
     values. Payload order is value for authored; coefficient, exponent for exact;
     coefficient, exponent, denominator for rational. Errors are [$ invalid_operand],
     or [$ resource_limit] for reached numeric guards. Deferred payload stays opaque.
  5. Recognized deferred domains fail [$ unsupported_domain], in operand order at
     their number/category path. A malformed right supported operand at stage 4
     precedes an unsupported left domain at stage 5.
  6. Division by mathematical zero is [$ division_by_zero] at [$ /input/right/number],
     before workspace. This applies to both [$ 0 / 0] and nonzero divided by zero,
     including authored negative zero. Next run the fixed workspace recipe, exact
     evaluation and canonical normalization. Check final coefficient, exponent,
     denominator limits in that order; resource failures here have null path.
     Materialization skips arithmetic evaluation and uses already admitted operands.
  7. Materialization checks representability, then emitted text-length allowance,
     then renders. [$ unrepresentable_result] precedes output [$ resource_limit];
     both have null path. No output-size guard substitutes for a pre-render check.

  Within each closed object check required fields in listed order before unknown
  fields. Unknown fields use decoded Unicode-scalar order, not host map order.
  Escape JSON Pointer tokens normally; if a pointer cannot be represented as a
  Unicode-scalar string, use null. Diagnostic message text is not normative.

  For authored [$ finiteNumber.value], after descriptor shape checks, use this
  separate stage-4 sequence. Every failure below uses the operand's
  [$ /input/left/number/value], [$ /input/right/number/value] or
  [$ /input/value/number/value] path, according to its position:

  1. Require text, otherwise [$ invalid_operand].
  2. Check the complete supplied text length against [$ maxNumericCharacters],
     including sign, point and exponent syntax; excess is [$ resource_limit].
  3. Validate the complete authored-number grammar from section 1; malformed text
     is [$ invalid_operand], before inspecting numerical exponent magnitude.
  4. Check the original authored exponent's absolute magnitude against
     [$ maxExponentMagnitude], treating an omitted exponent as zero. Ignore only
     exponent leading zeros for this magnitude comparison; do not convert an
     arbitrarily long exponent to a host integer. Excess is [$ resource_limit],
     even when the coefficient denotes zero.
  5. Determine the exact canonical coefficient/exponent pair without expanding
     decimal powers. Remove insignificant leading/trailing coefficient zeros and
     account for fractional digits in the normalized exponent. Mathematical zero
     becomes [$ (0,0)]. Check normalized coefficient digits first, then normalized
     exponent magnitude; either excess is [$ resource_limit]. Zero counts as one
     coefficient digit. Admit the normalized coefficient before converting it to
     an integer representation for calculation.

  Thus [$ 1.0] fits a coefficient allowance of one digit, while [$ 0e100] fails an
  authored-exponent allowance of two despite normalizing to zero. Authored [$ 1000]
  has one normalized coefficient digit but normalized exponent three, checked
  separately after its original omitted exponent zero. The text-length guard
  still applies to every authored character, including insignificant zeros.

  For derived payloads first check all required text types in listed order.
  Then check summed numeric-character allowance at the coefficient field. Check
  coefficient magnitude-text length (excluding one initial minus), then coefficient
  grammar. Check exponent grammar, then absolute magnitude without unbounded host
  conversion. For rational denominator check its text-length guard, then positive
  integer grammar, greater-than-one/no-two-or-five rule, then coprimality. Its last
  checks use the denominator path. Exact zero's nonzero exponent is an exponent-path
  error. These rules also apply to policy increment, using [$ invalid_policy] for
  invalid shapes/grammar/canonicality; a nonpositive increment fails coefficient.
  A wrong increment category fails its category path. Byte/numeric guards precede
  any expensive numerical conversion they protect.

  Examples: missing quantization mode precedes unsupported profile; invalid increment
  precedes malformed value; malformed left operand precedes a zero right divisor;
  valid zero divisor precedes insufficient arithmetic workspace; rational Number
  materialization fails representability even with zero rendering allowance.

  The portable contract is logical JSON. Native cycles fail closed with a detected
  [$ invalid_operand], or a reached admission [$ resource_limit] first. No cross-host
  traversal precedence is claimed for non-JSON cyclic graphs. Finite acyclic aliases
  remain subject to expanded structured-JSON bytes and per-path depth. Logical CTS
  does not establish bounded native getters/proxies, graph traversal or iterators.
  Native hardening needs separate evidence. Crashes/timeouts are harness failures.

  ## 7. Trusted limits [# 7-limits]

  Trusted host/adapter limits are outside the logical request. Invalid settings
  are harness/API failures, not operation diagnostics. Reject non-objects, null,
  unknown fields, Booleans and non-integer/negative values. The JSON binding admits
  exact integers through [$ 9007199254740991]. Omitted settings use configured defaults;
  every CTS execution supplies a complete explicit map. No native defaults or
  advertised capacities are standardized by this snapshot.

  - [$ maxRequestBytes]: UTF-8 logical JSON bytes, excluding the CTS envelope.
  - [$ maxRequestDepth]: nested container levels, root level one.
  - [$ maxNumericCharacters]: per operand or policy increment, authored value text
    or sum of supplied coefficient/exponent/denominator text lengths, including signs.
  - [$ maxCoefficientDigits]: decoded operand, increment and normalized result
    coefficient digits excluding minus. Zero counts as one.
  - [$ maxExponentMagnitude]: absolute authored exponent (even for zero), decoded
    normalized operand/increment exponent and normalized result exponent.
  - [$ maxDenominatorDigits]: admitted rational operand and normalized rational
    result denominator digits. Finite descriptors do not have synthetic denominators.
  - [$ maxWorkspaceDigits]: per-operation deterministic expanded-width admission
    from section 8; not total live memory, GCD iterations or elapsed time.
  - [$ maxOutputCharacters]: emitted Number literal text, including signs/point/
    exponent, excluding bundle/metadata. Derived results do not consume this allowance.

  Limits apply per operation; applications manage chain budgets. Final compactness
  does not waive required work guards. Positive CTS limits are explicit test settings,
  not promised Cartesian products of maxima or native support floors. Additional
  implementation execution budgets may be advertised separately and must not change
  the shared digit counts, silently round, omit required operations, or establish
  semantic conformance just by rejecting work. Positive cases require adequate declared
  execution allowances; execution-budget rejections are tested separately.

  ## 8. Portable workspace recipe [# 8-workspace]

  Let [$ L] be maxWorkspaceDigits. A required width [$ W] is admitted exactly when
  [$ W <= L]. For a nonzero operand write [$ (c, e, d)], with positive absolute
  coefficient [$ c], and [$ d = 1] for finite values. Signs are separate. [$ D(n)] is
  positive integer decimal digit count, including [$ D(1) = 1]. These conservative
  bounds need not equal actual private allocations. A product bound counts
  [$ D(a) + D(b)] even when a factor is one.

  After all input/domain checks, named shortcuts are addition/subtraction with zero,
  exact additive cancellation, multiplication by zero, zero numerator after division's
  zero-divisor check, and quantization of zero. They need no expanded workspace;
  final result/history rules still apply. Other identities do not waive admission.
  Bounded-factor GCD, factor extraction and input validation work on admitted integers
  before product guards. The width limit does not promise a generic GCD work bound.

  Multiplication requires cross-cancellation before its estimate:

  ~~~$ text
  g1 = gcd(cL, dR); g2 = gcd(cR, dL)
  A = cL/g1; B = cR/g2; C = dL/g2; D = dR/g1
  W = max(digits(A)+digits(B), digits(C)+digits(D))
  ~~~$

  Guard before forming numerator [$ A*B] and denominator [$ C*D]. They are relatively
  prime and denominator has no factors two/five. Strip numerator trailing zeros
  into exponent [$ eL+eR]. Final limits apply afterward. Thus [$ (1/999)*999] has
  required width two, not an uncancelled width four and not an identity width zero.

  Division checks zero first, then requires:

  ~~~$ text
  g1 = gcd(cL, cR); g2 = gcd(dR, dL)
  A = cL/g1; B = dR/g2; C = cR/g1; D = dL/g2
  a = v2(C); b = v5(C); C0 = C / (2^a * 5^b); t = max(a,b)
  P2(k) = floor(302*k/1000)+1; P5(k) = floor(699*k/1000)+1
  F = (P2(t-a) if t>a else 0) + (P5(t-b) if t>b else 0)
  W = max(digits(A)+digits(B)+F, digits(C0)+digits(D))
  ~~~$

  Here [$ v2], [$ v5] count exact factors; canonical [$ C] cannot have both. Guard
  before powers/products. Form numerator [$ A*B*2^(t-a)*5^(t-b)], denominator
  [$ C0*D], exponent [$ eL-eR-t], restoring sign. It is canonical. The power bounds
  are integer-only consequences of [$ 2^1000 < 10^302] and [$ 5^1000 < 10^699].
  For example, required widths are five for [$ 1/8], three for [$ 1/6], two for
  [$ 1e1000000/3]. The coefficients/results remain exact even with conservative bounds.

  Addition/subtraction requires least-common-denominator reduction first:

  ~~~$ text
  g = gcd(dL,dR); sL = dR/g; sR = dL/g; m = min(eL,eR)
  XL = digits(cL)+digits(sL)+eL-m
  XR = digits(cR)+digits(sR)+eR-m
  W = max(max(XL,XR)+1, digits(dL)+digits(sL))
  ~~~$

  Guard before products or exponent alignment. With operand signs [$ sigmaL],
  [$ sigmaR], form numerator
  [$ sigmaL*cL*sL*10^(eL-m) +/- sigmaR*cR*sR*10^(eR-m)], where the middle sign
  selects addition/subtraction; denominator is [$ dL*sL], exponent [$ m].
  Reduce and strip numerator trailing zeros. The extra digit reserves carry.
  [$ 1/999+2/999] has required width four, not the full-product estimate six.

  Quantization retains the same [$ L] throughout all reached phases:

  1. Form the compact exact internal ratio [$ abs(x)/h] with the division recipe.
     Its intermediates are not separately returned results: final coefficient,
     exponent and denominator limits do not apply to them as a separate operation.
     The operation's workspace guards do. Let its canonical triple be [$ (cQ,eQ,dQ)].
  2. Required on-grid shortcut: [$ dQ=1] and [$ eQ>=0] implies an integer ratio.
     Return the original normalized value, unchanged history and exact evaluation,
     without expanding an integer multiplier. Configuration/result checks still apply.
  3. Compare the ratio with one without decimal expansion. For positive scaled
     integers, compare adjusted digit counts [$ digits(a)+ea] and [$ digits(b)+eb].
     If equal, compare coefficient digits with virtual trailing zeros through only
     the longer coefficient text. Do not allocate exponent-proportional padding.
  4. Required sub-unit shortcut for [$ 0<abs(x)/h<1]: guard width
     [$ max(digits(cQ)+1,digits(dQ))] before doubling the coefficient. Compare
     [$ 2*cQ*10^eQ] with [$ dQ] using that virtual rule. Select multiplier zero/one
     with mode/sign, return zero or signed increment, and mark rounded/history.
     This comparison width is already covered by the finite-increment ratio bound.
  5. Otherwise guard [$ max(digits(cQ)+max(eQ,0),digits(dQ)+max(-eQ,0))] before
     expanding numerator [$ N] or denominator [$ D] by the corresponding power ten.
     Compute [$ n,r=divmod(N,D)]. For nearest modes compare [$ r] with [$ D-r], not
     an unnecessarily expanded doubled remainder. Select multiplier [$ k<=N].
  6. For nonzero [$ k], guard [$ digits(k)+digits(increment.coefficient)] before
     the grid product. Zero needs no grid-product expansion. Restore sign, normalize
     and apply final result/history/metadata rules. No independent budget reset occurs.

  All configured limits apply where reached. A transient internal ratio exponent is
  not a normalized output exponent and is controlled by the relevant work checks.
  Required compact shortcuts admit huge authored decimal powers without expanding
  them solely to recover an unchanged value, zero or one increment. They do not
  promise every small final result has small required workspace.

  ## 9. Snapshot conformance boundary [# 9-conformance]

  The full six-operation profile is one semantic capability, not optional per-operation
  flags. Separate CTS binding, suites and runner exercise authored/derived pairings,
  actual-result chains, all rounding modes/signs/ties, canonical descriptors, policy
  composition, staged diagnostics and resource boundary companions. A declared
  capacity cannot masquerade as excluding rational operands or an operation.

  The parallel aeonic-rational-arithmetic-cts-v1-snapshot-0.2 is the immutable
  executable compatibility target. Source, schema, binding, suites and shared
  runner dependencies are content-pinned and rechecked after execution.
  Artifact-only validation runs no SUT and must not be reported as a semantic or
  stable conformance pass. The separate mutable next target remains development
  evidence. Independent implementations retain exact revisions and explicit
  selected limits; the snapshot does not promise every Cartesian combination of
  maxima or a machine-time/memory bound. Existing finite/comparison claims need
  their own unchanged frozen regressions. No package publication, new default,
  fraction literal or application convenience result contract is introduced.
`
